\section*{Appendix E: Autonomous Intelligence Looping Algorithms}

\subsection*{E.1 Closed-Loop Correction Cycle}
The autonomous intelligence kernel operates through iterative correction cycles driven
by residual error feedback. At each timestep $t$, the system computes:

\begin{equation}
\epsilon(t) =
\left\|
\mathcal{F}(t) - \mathcal{F}_{\text{pred}}(t)
\right\|,
\end{equation}

where $\mathcal{F}(t)$ is the fused telemetry tensor and 
$\mathcal{F}_{\text{pred}}(t)$ is the predicted tensor from the previous iteration.

If $\epsilon(t) > \tau$, the kernel triggers a correction cycle:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}^{(n+1)} =
\mathbf{X}^{(n)} -
\alpha \cdot \nabla_{\mathbf{X}} \epsilon(t),
\end{equation}

where $\alpha$ is the adaptive learning rate.

\subsection*{E.2 Adaptive Solver Precision}
Solver precision is dynamically adjusted according to the magnitude of the residual:

\begin{equation}
p(t+1) =
\begin{cases}
p(t) + \Delta p, & \epsilon(t) > \tau, \\
p(t) - \Delta p, & \epsilon(t) < \tau/10,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

ensuring computational efficiency while maintaining physical accuracy.

\subsection*{E.3 Autonomous Grid Resolution Control}
Grid resolution is modified using the refinement and coarsening operators defined in
Appendix D. The intelligence kernel selects the appropriate operator based on:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}(i,j,t) =
\begin{cases}
\mathcal{R}, & \Delta_{i,j}(t) > \tau, \\
\mathcal{C}, & \Delta_{i,j}(t) < \tau/10, \\
\text{Identity}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

This ensures local adaptation to high-gradient regions.

\subsection*{E.4 Telemetry-Driven Hamiltonian Updates}
The multi-physics Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$ is updated using telemetry-derived correction
terms:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}(t+1) =
\mathcal{H}(t) +
\beta \cdot
\left(
\mathcal{F}(t) - \mathcal{F}_{\text{pred}}(t)
\right),
\end{equation}

where $\beta$ is a stability coefficient.  
This enables real-time alignment between simulation and observational data.

